1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,352
Choose a clear tracing and measure from QRS onset to the end of T,
where it returns to the baseline. Distinguish a separate U wave. When
T and U merge or the endpoint is uncertain, record that limitation
rather than inventing an exact duration.

2
00:00:17,352 --> 00:00:36,240
QT varies with heart rate, so also measure a representative RR
interval. Document the lead, rate, raw QT and correction method when
available. A machine-generated QTc is a useful check but still depends
on correct detection and a valid signal.

3
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:58,536
For the school Bazett calculation, divide QT by the square root of RR
in seconds. In this original worked example, QT is zero point four two
zero seconds and RR is zero point six four seconds. The square root is
zero point eight; corrected QT is zero point five two five seconds.

4
00:00:58,536 --> 00:01:16,128
Convert that result to five hundred twenty-five milliseconds.
Alternatively use QT in milliseconds with RR still in seconds, giving
a millisecond result. Do not put RR in milliseconds under the square
root, and do not label a seconds result as milliseconds.

5
00:01:16,128 --> 00:01:33,912
The school uses upper reference values of four hundred fifty
milliseconds for men and four hundred sixty for women, with more than
five hundred a major warning. These are course reference cutoffs, not
a boundary that makes every lower result safe or every higher result a
complete diagnosis.

6
00:01:33,912 --> 00:01:54,936
Bazett can miscorrect at very fast or slow rates. Variable RR,
uncertain T endpoints and a wide QRS further complicate assessment.
Repeat when appropriate, compare prior tracings and seek expert
interpretation instead of allowing a borderline automated number to
dictate a high-risk decision alone.

7
00:01:54,936 --> 00:02:12,168
The school names amiodarone, ondansetron, haloperidol and sotalol,
along with some antibiotics and antifungals. The point is to review
the actual medication list, combinations and patient risk, not to
assume that only cardiac drugs affect ventricular recovery.

8
00:02:12,168 --> 00:02:32,280
Ask about prescribed and nonprescribed drugs, recent additions,
vomiting or diarrhoea, renal problems and known long-QT disease.
Suspected electrolyte depletion or several QT-prolonging drugs can
compound risk. Communicate the concern and consult the applicable
prescribing or medical-control pathway.

9
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:53,784
The school emphasizes baseline ECG review before non-time-critical
exposure to QT-prolonging medication. Read that alongside the current
indication, local drug monograph and your licence level. Do not
independently discontinue a patient’s prescriptions or substitute a
drug merely because a teaching threshold was crossed.

10
00:02:53,784 --> 00:03:13,824
Context changes priorities. A pre-infusion safety check is not a
reason to postpone CPR or indicated defibrillation during arrest. The
warning that a drug may prolong QT does not replace a rhythm-specific
emergency pathway, and this chapter supplies no independent PCP dosing
authority.

11
00:03:13,824 --> 00:03:31,296
Torsades de pointes is a form of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia
associated with a prolonged QT in the underlying rhythm. The complexes
appear to grow, shrink and rotate direction. It is different from the
uniform appearance of monomorphic VT.

12
00:03:31,296 --> 00:03:50,352
Not every polymorphic VT is torsades. A normal-QT polymorphic rhythm
may have a different mechanism, including ischemia. Report what was
seen and any preceding QT information; do not delay emergency
treatment while searching for a previous interval that is not
available.

13
00:03:50,352 --> 00:04:12,096
Assess the patient immediately. Current resuscitation guidance treats
sustained polymorphic VT as an unstable rhythm requiring an immediate
unsynchronized-shock response by authorized personnel. If pulseless,
provide CPR and indicated defibrillation through the applicable
pathway rather than waiting to calculate QTc.

14
00:04:12,096 --> 00:04:31,320
Magnesium can be relevant to recurrence of long-QT torsades; it is not
a substitute for terminating an immediately dangerous sustained
rhythm, and it is not a universal treatment for every polymorphic VT.
Expert medication and pacing decisions must follow current medical
direction and scope.

15
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:50,304
A useful report includes raw QT and RR, the corrected value with
units, the method and any measurement limitation. Add symptoms such as
syncope, the relevant medication history and whether there was a
rhythm change. This is more useful than saying only that the QT looks
long.

16
00:04:50,304 --> 00:05:08,952
Document whom you consulted and the plan reached through the
authorized pathway. Repeat values and symptom trends help the
receiving team understand changing risk. A teaching threshold should
produce deliberate assessment and communication, not an undocumented
automatic medication decision.
